          "ruleId": "review-note",
          "ruleIndex": 0,
          "kind": "informational",
          "level": "none",
          "guid": "mt3jx39hcoegb4k5",
          "message": {
            "text": "Ordinary URL backgrounds stay vector-owned because Blink completes CSS tile geometry before Skia sampling. Pinned Chromium BackgroundImageGeometry supplies natural sizing, snapped/unsnapped areas, destination, phase, spacing, and scale; pinned Skia only consumes the resulting subset, matrix, and tile modes. This split prevents a whole-box raster fallback from hiding a representable geometry defect.",
            "markdown": "Ordinary URL backgrounds stay vector-owned because Blink completes CSS tile geometry before Skia sampling. Pinned Chromium BackgroundImageGeometry supplies natural sizing, snapped/unsnapped areas, destination, phase, spacing, and scale; pinned Skia only consumes the resulting subset, matrix, and tile modes. This split prevents a whole-box raster fallback from hiding a representable geometry defect."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "docs/163-url-background-image-geometry-audit.md"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 21,
                  "endLine": 43,
                  "snippet": {
                    "text": "The source-derived contract has four stages:\n\n1. **Capture the selected CSS image and its complete natural-sizing facts.**\n   The facts are not just two positive integers. They include the selected\n   `image-set()` candidate and resolution, independently present natural\n   dimensions, natural aspect ratio, orientation, effective zoom, and a\n   decoded/failure state. Unknown sizing must be explicit; it must not silently\n   become the positioning-area dimensions.\n2. **Resolve one Blink-equivalent tile geometry record per expanded layer.**\n   The record owns unsnapped and snapped positioning/painting rectangles, tile\n   size, destination, phase, repeat spacing, source subset/scale, origin and\n   clip boxes, and attachment/fragment offsets. Shorter background longhand\n   lists repeat cyclically; they do not fall back permanently to item zero.\n3. **Keep ordinary URL images on the vector composition path.** A generated\n   SVG can represent the resulting destination/clip/pattern matrix exactly.\n   Bitmap sources remain bitmap `<image>` payloads and SVG sources may remain\n   scalable image payloads, but the surrounding element does not need a\n   Chromium screenshot merely because its background tiles.\n4. **Treat Skia as the consumer, not the geometry oracle.** Blink has already\n   chosen tile size, phase, spacing, subset, destination, and scale before\n   Skia creates its image shader. Skia validates the subset/matrix and executes\n   clamp/repeat sampling. Reconstructing geometry from a plausible SVG repeat\n   cell after the fact is the wrong ownership direction."
                  }
                }
              }
            }
          ],
          "properties": {
            "tags": [
              "rationale"
            ],
            "ext-ai-tool-confidence": 0.98
          },
